::STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.2
Question:
In the model y = 6x + 10, what is the slope?

{
=A. 6#Correct. The slope is the coefficient of x, so each 1-unit increase in x changes the prediction by 6.
~B. 10#This is the y-intercept, not the slope.
~C. 16#This adds the slope and intercept, which does not identify the slope.
~D. x#The variable x is the input, not the slope.
}

::STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.2
Question:
In y = 6x + 10, what does the y-intercept mean if x is hours and y is cost?

{
~A. The cost increases by 6 dollars each hour#That describes the slope, not the y-intercept.
=B. The predicted starting cost is 10 dollars when x = 0#Correct. The y-intercept is the predicted value when the input is 0.
~C. The total cost is always 6 dollars#The model changes with x, so cost is not always 6 dollars.
~D. The model cannot make predictions#A linear model can make predictions within a reasonable range.
}

::STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.2
Question:
Use y = 4x + 30 to predict y when x = 5.

{
~A. 34#This substitutes incorrectly; 4 times 5 is 20, not 4.
~B. 35#This adds 5 to 30 but does not multiply 4 by 5 first.
=C. 50, because 4(5) + 30 = 50#Correct. Substitute 5 for x and simplify.
~D. 150#This multiplies 30 by 5 and ignores the model structure.
}

::STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.2
Question:
Why can prediction far outside the data range be risky?

{
~A. The slope disappears outside the data range#The slope does not disappear, but the model may not remain appropriate.
~B. The y-intercept becomes the only useful value#The intercept does not replace the whole model.
~C. Equations cannot use large x-values#Equations can use large values, but the context may make the prediction unreliable.
=D. The relationship may not continue the same way#Correct. Extrapolation assumes the pattern continues beyond the observed data.
}

::STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.2
Question:
Which scatterplot pattern best supports a linear model?

{
=A. Points that cluster around a straight-line pattern#Correct. A linear model is appropriate when the relationship is roughly straight.
~B. Points forming a circle#A circular pattern is not well modeled by a line.
~C. Points with no visible pattern#No visible pattern does not support a useful linear model.
~D. Points that alternate randomly up and down#Random alternating points do not show a clear straight-line relationship.
}
